  "content": {
    "id": "v2-de45f-p12-practice-slot-4-2-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Calculate the values of $t$ if the distance between $(t,-t)$ and $(3,1)$ is exactly $\\sqrt{26}$ units.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$t=4$ or $-2$. Square the distance equation and factorise the resulting quadratic.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\sqrt{(3-t)^2+(1-(-t))^2}&=\\sqrt{26}\\\\(3-t)^2+(1+t)^2&=26\\\\9-6t+t^2+1+2t+t^2&=26\\\\2t^2-4t+10&=26\\\\2t^2-4t-16&=0\\\\t^2-2t-8&=0\\end{align*}$$PSF: Product $=-8$, Sum $=-2$, Factors $=2$ and $-4$.$$\\begin{align*}(t+2)(t-4)&=0\\\\t&=-2\\text{ or }4\\end{align*}$$Check $t=4$: $\\sqrt{1^2+(-5)^2}=\\sqrt{26}$. Check $t=-2$: $\\sqrt{(-5)^2+1^2}=\\sqrt{26}$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": null
  },
